<description>
`sendall` keeps the recipients that were given without an amount as the strings the user passed (spend.cpp:1399). Later it finds their outputs by comparing against `EncodeDestination` of each output script (spend.cpp:1564), which for bech32 is the canonical lowercase form.

An address given in uppercase is valid bech32, and `validateaddress` and `sendtoaddress` accept it. In `sendall` it never matches, so its output keeps the placeholder amount of 0 and the call fails:

```
sendall '["BCRT1Q..."]'
error code: -8
Specified output amount to bcrt1q... is below dust threshold.
```

This stores the decoded destinations of those addresses instead and compares them with the destinations of the outputs, so the match doesn't depend on how the address was written. Invalid addresses are still rejected later when the transaction is constructed.

The new `sendall_uppercase_address` in `wallet_sendall.py` sweeps the wallet to the uppercase form of an address. It fails on master with the dust error above and passes with the fix. `wallet_send.py` still passes.
</description>

<commits>
df2994b14a wallet: accept uppercase addresses without amount in sendall

sendall remembers the recipients given without an amount as the strings
the user passed, and later finds their outputs by comparing against
EncodeDestination of each output script, which is the canonical
lowercase form for bech32. An address given in uppercase, which is
valid bech32 and accepted by the rest of the wallet, never matches. Its
output then keeps the placeholder amount of 0 and sendall fails with
"Specified output amount to bcrt1q... is below dust threshold."

Store the decoded destinations of those addresses instead and compare
them with the destinations of the outputs, so the match no longer
depends on how the address was written.
</commits>

